<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jaimal Rathore</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1568">1568</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Jaimal Mertiya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jaimal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathore</a>, also called Jaimal Mertiya, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> chief of Merta who commanded the defence of Chittor when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> besieged the fort in 1567–1568, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udai-singh-ii">Udai Singh II</a> had withdrawn to the hills. Wounded during the siege, according to Mughal accounts by a shot from Akbar himself, he died in the final assault in February 1568. Akbar later had statues of Jaimal and his fellow commander Patta set up at the gate of the fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jaimal-rathore.jpg" alt="Jaimal Rathore" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Beglar, Joseph David (1845-1907) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaimal_phatta_delhi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
